        <div id="waveform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pure Sine vs Modified Sine: Which Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How the Two Waveforms Differ</h3>

          <p>
            Every inverter converts DC from a battery into AC, but they do not all produce the same AC. A pure sine wave inverter produces a smooth waveform that matches, and often beats, the quality of utility power. A modified sine wave inverter produces a stepped approximation that switches abruptly between levels. We cover the electrical details in our guide to <a href="/pure-sine-wave-vs-modified-sine-wave/">pure sine wave vs modified sine wave</a>; here is what it means for buying an inverter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Modified Sine Works</h3>

          <p>
            Modified sine wave is adequate for resistive loads and simple motors. Kettles, space heaters, incandescent bulbs, older coffee makers, and universal-motor tools such as circular saws and angle grinders run fine. Most phone and laptop chargers work, though some run hot or buzz. Modified sine units cost substantially less, which is why budget 2,000W inverters at big-box prices are almost all modified sine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Pure Sine Is Required</h3>

          <p>
            Pure sine wave is required for a long and growing list of devices: CPAP machines, especially with heated humidifiers, variable-speed tools, induction cooktops, microwaves, which run but deliver less cooking power and hum on modified sine, refrigerators and other induction motors, which run hotter and less efficiently, some cordless tool battery chargers, LED drivers, audio equipment and anything with a digital clock or timer, which may run fast. Some of these devices fail outright or wear out faster on modified sine power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Buy, and Vague Listings</h3>

          <p>
            Our advice is simple. If anything on your list is medical, electronic beyond a phone charger, or contains a compressor or speed-controlled motor, buy pure sine. The price gap has narrowed enough that modified sine only makes sense for a work truck running tools and lights, or a plug-in car inverter charging laptops and phones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beware listings that are vague about waveform. Some budget brands sell modified and pure sine versions under nearly identical names, and a 2,000W modified sine unit is often listed beside a pure sine model at a very different price. If the spec sheet does not say pure sine wave explicitly, assume it is modified.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Size an Inverter: 1000W, 1500W, 2000W or 4000W
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start With Running and Surge Watts</h3>

          <p>
            Inverter sizing starts with the single largest load you want to run, plus anything else that will run at the same time. Add up the running watts, then check the starting surge of anything with a motor or compressor. Choose an inverter whose continuous rating is at least 20 to 25 percent above your total running load and whose surge rating covers the biggest motor start.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Running Watts</p>
