          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choosing a Size Class</h3>

          <p>
            A 1,500W inverter is a reasonable middle ground for vans that want a kettle or a small microwave but not both at once. The 2,000W class is the most popular because it covers nearly every single kitchen appliance. Above 3,000W you are building an off-grid electrical system, not adding an accessory, and the design should move to a higher battery voltage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Not the Same as an Inverter Generator</h3>

          <p>
            Do not confuse a power inverter with an inverter generator. An inverter generator is a gas generator that uses inverter electronics to produce clean power; it makes its own energy from fuel. A power inverter only converts energy stored in a battery. If you need power for days rather than hours, our guide to the <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">best inverter generators</a> is the place to look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bigger Is Not Free</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, bigger is not free. A larger inverter draws more idle current even with nothing plugged in, often 1 to 2 amps for a 2,000W unit, which can drain 30Ah to 50Ah from a battery in a day. Buy the size you need, and use power save or ECO mode when it is available.
          </p>
