        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Battery Bank Matters More Than the Inverter
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Current Draw</h3>

          <p>
            An inverter stores no energy. Every watt it delivers comes from the battery, plus 10 to 15 percent more for conversion losses. The formula for the current draw is straightforward: output watts divided by battery voltage divided by efficiency. A 2,000W load on a 12V system at 90 percent efficiency draws about 185 amps. That number drives every other decision.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity and Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Battery capacity determines runtime. A 100Ah 12V LiFePO4 battery stores about 1,280Wh. At 90 percent inverter efficiency and with the battery taken close to empty, it can deliver roughly 1,150Wh of AC, which runs a 1,500W microwave for about 45 minutes in total, a 60W CPAP for about 19 hours or a 2,000W load for about 35 minutes. Lead-acid and AGM batteries should only be discharged to about 50 percent for reasonable life, so a 100Ah AGM battery delivers only about half that, and less still at high currents.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">BMS Current Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Battery chemistry also determines whether the battery can deliver the current at all. Many 100Ah lithium batteries have a battery management system limited to 100A continuous, which caps them at roughly 1,100W to 1,200W of inverter output. Running a 2,000W inverter at full load needs either a battery rated for 200A continuous or two 100A batteries in parallel. Exceed the BMS limit and the battery simply shuts off, taking the inverter with it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Batteries and the Power Station Alternative</h3>

          <p>
            A car or truck starting battery is the wrong battery for anything but brief use with the engine running. Starting batteries are designed for short, high-current bursts, not deep discharge, and running one flat with an inverter can leave you stranded and shorten the battery's life sharply. For regular use, install a separate deep-cycle house battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            If assembling a battery bank, inverter, charger and wiring sounds like more project than you want, a power station packages all of it in one box. We compare the two approaches below, and our <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion</a> guide explains why LiFePO4 has become the default chemistry for deep-cycle use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cable Gauge, Fusing and Installation
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Cable Size Matters</h3>

          <p>
            High-current DC wiring is where most inverter installations go wrong. At 185 amps, undersized cable heats up, drops voltage and triggers the inverter's low-voltage shutdown under load, which owners usually blame on the inverter. In the worst case, an unfused cable shorted to the vehicle frame can start a fire. Use the cable gauge your inverter's manual specifies, keep the runs as short as possible and always fuse the positive cable close to the battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cable Gauge by Inverter Size</h3>

          <p>
            The table below shows common manufacturer recommendations for 12V inverters on short runs of about six feet or less between battery and inverter. Longer runs need heavier cable. Your inverter's manual is the authority; if it differs from this table, follow the manual.
          </p>

