                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">350-400A+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Class T</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fusing and Disconnects</h3>

          <p>
            Fuse within about 7 inches of the battery positive terminal where possible. For lithium batteries, which can deliver enormous short-circuit currents, a Class T fuse is the safer choice because its interrupt rating is far higher than an ANL fuse. Add a battery disconnect switch so you can isolate the inverter for service. Use pure copper cable with properly crimped lugs, not copper-clad aluminum and not lugs hammered on with a punch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mounting, Grounding and RV Outlets</h3>

          <p>
            Mount the inverter in a dry, ventilated location close to the battery but not in the same sealed box as a flooded lead-acid battery, which vents hydrogen. Leave clearance around the fans. Connect the inverter's chassis ground lug to the vehicle chassis or the system's ground bus with cable of the size the manual specifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the inverter to power an RV's built-in outlets, you need a transfer switch that selects between shore power, a generator and the inverter, or an inverter charger with one built in, such as the AIMS unit on our list. Never connect an inverter's output to a circuit that can also receive shore or grid power without one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="socket" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Car Cigarette Socket Limits: Why 150W Is the Real Ceiling
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Socket Caps Out</h3>

          <p>
            Plug-in inverters are sold with ratings of 300W, 400W and even 500W, but a car's 12V accessory socket cannot deliver that. The socket circuit is protected by a fuse, usually 10A, 15A or occasionally 20A. At 12V, a 10A circuit supplies about 120W and a 15A circuit about 180W, before inverter losses. That is why we tell buyers to plan on roughly 150W through a cigarette socket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try to pull more and you will blow the vehicle's accessory fuse, which often also feeds other circuits such as the radio or USB ports, or the inverter will shut down on low voltage because the thin socket wiring drops too much voltage under load. Repeatedly overloading a socket can also overheat the socket itself. Check your owner's manual or fuse box diagram for the actual rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Fits Within 150W</h3>

          <p>
            Within that 150W, a plug-in inverter is genuinely useful. Laptop chargers draw 45W to 100W, camera and drone battery chargers 30W to 80W, and phones a few watts. A breast pump, a small fan or an electric cooler on its AC adapter usually fits. A hair dryer, kettle, coffee maker or microwave does not, no matter what the inverter's label says.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Going Beyond the Socket</h3>

          <p>
            For more than about 150W, connect the inverter directly to the battery with appropriately sized cables and a fuse, as the Bestek's included clamps allow for its full 300W. For anything above roughly 400W to 500W, a permanently wired installation with proper cable and fusing is the only safe approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many newer trucks and SUVs include a factory 120V outlet, typically rated 150W in cars and up to 400W in some trucks, with a few hybrid and electric pickups offering several kilowatts. If your vehicle has one, use it before buying an aftermarket inverter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vehicle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running an Inverter Off a Vehicle Alternator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Alternator Output at Idle</h3>

          <p>
            Running an inverter with the engine on is a common way to power tools or appliances from a truck, and it works within limits. Most passenger vehicle alternators are rated somewhere between 120 and 220 amps, but much of that is consumed by the vehicle itself, and alternators produce much less at idle than at driving speed. A 2,000W inverter at full load needs about 185 amps, which is more than most stock alternators can supply at idle.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, the alternator keeps up with moderate loads, a few hundred watts, while the battery covers short bursts of heavy use like a microwave or a saw cut. Sustained high loads at idle will slowly drain the starting battery even with the engine running. Owners of work trucks that run inverters all day often fit a high-output alternator for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a House Battery and DC-DC Charger</h3>

          <p>
            For a camper van or overlanding rig, the better design is a separate house battery charged from the alternator through a DC-DC charger. The DC-DC charger limits the current drawn from the alternator, charges the house battery with the correct profile for its chemistry and isolates it so the inverter can never flatten the starting battery. Lithium house batteries in particular should be charged this way, because they can draw more current than an alternator can safely supply for long.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Engine-Off and Idling Risks</h3>

          <p>
            Do not leave a large inverter on with the engine off and no separate house battery. Even idle current can drain a starting battery overnight, and many inverters' low-voltage cutoff is set below the point where the engine will still crank reliably. If you only need power occasionally with the engine off, a portable power station removes the risk entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Idling an engine for long periods to run an inverter is also inefficient. A typical gasoline engine burns roughly half a gallon or more per hour at idle, and a small inverter generator will produce the same power on far less fuel, with no wear on the vehicle engine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Inverter vs Portable Power Station
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Power Station Bundles</h3>

          <p>
            A portable power station is an inverter, a battery, a battery management system, a charger and a solar charge controller in one box, with no cables to size and no fuses to choose. For many buyers that is the better product. Our ranking of the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> covers the leading models, and many current 1,000Wh units deliver 1,800W to 2,400W of pure sine output.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Standalone Inverter Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a standalone inverter when you already have a battery bank, such as an RV or van house battery, when you need more continuous power or capacity than a portable unit offers for the money, or when the system will be permanently installed and charged from an alternator and solar. Per watt-hour, a lithium battery plus an inverter is usually cheaper than a power station of the same capacity, especially above about 2,000Wh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Power Station Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a power station when you want portability, when you do not want to do DC wiring, or when the power is for occasional use such as camping, a CPAP or household backup during outages. Power stations also recharge from a wall outlet in an hour or two and accept solar directly. Our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best power station for camping</a> and the <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generators</a> cover those uses.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cost Picture and Home Backup</h3>

          <p>
            The cost picture is closer than it looks. A quality 2,000W pure sine inverter, 200Ah of lithium, a DC-DC charger, cables, a Class T fuse and a disconnect add up quickly, and that is before a solar controller. A power station of similar capacity and output is often within a few hundred dollars and takes minutes to set up rather than a weekend.
          </p>

          <p>
            For home backup specifically, neither an inverter on a car battery nor an extension cord through a window is a good plan for anything beyond a few small loads. Our comparison of a <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a> covers the options for keeping a refrigerator, furnace fan and lights running through a long outage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety: GFCI, Grounding, Ventilation and Backfeeding
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Certification</h3>

          <p>
            An inverter produces the same 120V AC as a wall outlet, and it can injure or kill in the same way. Buy an inverter listed to UL 458 for vehicle, RV and marine installations, or at minimum one with ETL or UL certification, rather than an uncertified unit. Many of the cheapest no-name inverters carry no real certification and have optimistic ratings to match.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">GFCI in Wet Locations</h3>

          <p>
            Use GFCI protection in wet environments. Several inverters, including the Go Power GP-SW series, include GFCI outlets. If yours does not and you are using it outdoors, in a boat or near water, plug in a portable GFCI adapter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Backfeed the House</h3>

          <p>
            Never connect an inverter to your house wiring by plugging it into a wall outlet with a double-ended cord. This backfeeds the house circuit, can energize the utility lines that line workers believe are dead and can start a fire. The only safe way to power home circuits from an inverter or generator is through a transfer switch or an interlock kit. Our guide to the <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switches</a> explains the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilation and Switching Off</h3>

          <p>
            Keep inverters ventilated and away from fuel. Inverters get hot under load and their fans pull in air from around them; do not bury one under bedding or bags or mount it in the same compartment as gasoline or propane. Lead-acid batteries vent hydrogen while charging, so keep them in a vented box separate from the inverter's electronics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the inverter switched off when not in use. Even idle current adds up, and a forgotten inverter is the most common reason a camper wakes to a flat battery. A remote switch mounted where you will see it, included with the Renogy units, makes this easy.
          </p>
        </div>

