        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 2000 watt power inverter run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2,000W inverter runs most single kitchen appliances, including a full-size microwave drawing about 1,500W, a drip coffee maker, an electric kettle or a toaster oven, plus small loads at the same time. It also runs a circular saw, a small window air conditioner or a refrigerator. It needs a battery bank able to supply about 185 amps continuously.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 12V battery run a 2000W inverter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable battery energy by the load, allowing about 10 percent for losses. A 100Ah 12V lithium battery stores about 1,280Wh, so at a full 2,000W load it lasts roughly 35 minutes, if its BMS can deliver the current. At a 500W average load, it lasts a little over two hours. A lead-acid battery of the same rating delivers about half that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 1000W inverter enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most vans and campers, yes. A 1,000W inverter runs laptops, a CPAP, a TV, a small blender, tool battery chargers and a portable fridge. It will not reliably run a full-size microwave, most coffee makers, kettles or hair dryers, which draw 1,200W to 1,800W. Its main advantage is needing about half the battery current and cable size of a 2,000W unit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a pure sine wave inverter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you will run a CPAP, laptops, a microwave, a refrigerator, variable-speed tools, induction cooktops or audio gear, yes. Those devices can run hot, hum or fail on modified sine power. Modified sine is fine for heaters, kettles, incandescent lights and simple power tools, which is why budget work-truck inverters still use it. When in doubt, buy pure sine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size cable do I need for a 2000W inverter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers specify 1/0 AWG copper cable for a 2,000W 12V inverter on a short run of about six feet or less, and 2/0 for longer runs, with a fuse of about 250A close to the battery. Always follow your inverter's manual. Keep the battery and inverter as close together as practical, since every extra foot adds voltage drop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug an inverter into my car's cigarette lighter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for small loads. Car accessory sockets are typically fused at 10 to 15 amps, which supports roughly 120W to 180W. That is enough for laptops, phones and camera chargers. For anything larger, connect the inverter directly to the battery with properly sized cables and a fuse, and run the engine to avoid draining the starting battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power inverter better than a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on what you already have. If you have a battery bank in an RV or van, a quality inverter is cheaper per watt and can be permanently installed. If you want plug-and-play power for camping, CPAP or home outages without DC wiring, a portable power station is simpler and includes the battery, charger and solar input in one box.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a big inverter and connecting it to a small battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2,000W inverter draws about 185 amps at full load. A single starting battery or a 100Ah lithium battery with a 100A BMS limit cannot supply that, and the inverter or battery shuts down the moment you start the microwave. Size the battery bank and its continuous current rating before you buy the inverter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Undersized cables and no fuse</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin cables drop voltage, heat up and trigger low-voltage shutdowns that owners blame on the inverter. An unfused cable shorted to a vehicle frame can start a fire. Use the gauge your manual specifies, keep runs short, and put a properly rated ANL or Class T fuse within inches of the battery positive terminal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting 300W from a cigarette socket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Car accessory sockets are typically fused at 10 to 15 amps, which works out to roughly 120W to 180W. A 300W or 500W plug-in inverter will blow the vehicle fuse or shut down if you try to use its full rating through the socket. Plan on about 150W, or clamp directly to the battery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a CPAP or laptop on modified sine wave</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Modified sine inverters can make CPAP humidifiers, some laptop chargers, variable-speed tools and induction motors run hot, hum or fail. The savings over pure sine are small compared with the cost of a damaged medical device. If anything on your list is electronic or medical, buy a pure sine wave inverter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding a house through a wall outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging an inverter into a wall outlet with a double-ended cord energizes the house wiring and can send power back onto utility lines, endangering line workers and risking fire. Powering home circuits requires a transfer switch or interlock kit installed to code. For outages, plug appliances into the inverter directly or use a proper switch.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

